    description: "FOUND 2026-09-01 by the module-key concept audit (cadence). Full write-up:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/concept-audit-module-key_09012026.md\n\nTHE DEFECT. _module_matches has four arms. Arm 3, \"dropped qualification\"\n(cm_parts[-shared:] == em_parts[-shared:]), carries NO capitalisation test, and\nits in-code justification is that \"the extra components are leading NAMESPACE,\nand dropping a namespace cannot turn one module into a different one the way\nappending a sub-package can\". That holds when the trailing component is\nnamespace. It fails when the catalogue module's trailing component is a TYPE:\nthe arm then matches a bare receiver-VARIABLE name of the same spelling.\n\nVERIFIED LIVE, END TO END, through the production classifier on sops\n(curriculum_repos/sops, cmd/sops/main.go:2383):\n\n    func inputStore(context *cli.Context, path string) (common.Store, error) {\n        ...\n        return common.DefaultStoreForPathOrFormat(\n            storesConf, path, context.String(\"input-type\")), nil\n\n`context` is a PARAMETER VARIABLE of type *cli.Context (urfave/cli);\ncontext.String(\"input-type\") reads a CLI flag. Production says:\n\n    classify_call_in_catalog(module_path=\"context\", name=\"String\",\n                             meta={call_construct: method}) ->\n      IoPrimitive(boundary='net_send',\n                  module='github.com/gin-gonic/gin.Context',\n                  name='String', kind='method', unvouched=True,\n                  notes=\"...whatever reaches an argument leaves the process.\")\n\n    CONTROL: context.Deadline -> None      (the instrument discriminates)\n\nA CLI flag read is modelled as leaving the process over HTTP. net_send is a\ntaint SINK and unvouched rows are permitted to produce DETECTIONS (they are\nbarred only from licensing a CLEAN verdict), so this is the false-detection\ndirection.\n\nTHIS IS INV-safig's REMEDY BITING BACK, and that is the part worth keeping.\nINV-safig found gin.Context unreachable from the real import path; the fix\nrewrote the module to github.com/gin-gonic/gin.Context, which arm 2\n(prefix+capital) then rescues correctly. But appending the type made Context\nthe TRAILING component, which arm 3 matches against a bare `context`. Both\ndirections verified on the shipped catalogue:\n\n    lookup('String', 'github.com/gin-gonic/gin') -> gin.Context.String  # intended\n    lookup('String', 'context')                  -> gin.Context.String  # FALSE POSITIVE\n\nSo the row moved from \"unreachable\" to \"reachable from the right hint AND from\na wrong one\". INV-safig stays satisfied on its own statement; this is its\nresidual, filed separately rather than reopening it.\n\nEXPOSURE. 1,090 catalogue rows across 9 languages have a multi-component module\nwhose last component is capitalised, so each is matchable from a bare lowercase\nhint equal to its type name. The colliding tokens are the commonest variable\nnames there are: python Path 40 rows, kotlin File 34, elixir Repo 24, haskell\nByteString 23, rust File 23, go Context 13.\n\nHARM MEASURED, BOTH NUMBERS. Cohort: 21 repos, 10 languages, every repo cold in\nits own XDG_CACHE_HOME. Of 1,676 tagged boundaries the two heuristic arms decide\n32 (1.9%) -- prefix+capital 25, suffix 7. All 9 distinct shapes hand-adjudicated:\n8 reach the right boundary, 1 is wrong (this one, 2 calls). So the defect is\nLOW-VOLUME on this cohort and REAL. Two of the 8 correct ones are correct by\nluck, not design: elixir Task <- Task.Supervisor and haskell Data.ByteString <-\nData.ByteString.Lazy are sibling MODULES matched as though Supervisor/Lazy were\ntypes, which happen to share the boundary of the row they hit.\n\nROOT CAUSE BELOW THE ARM. Arm 2 decides package-vs-type ORTHOGRAPHICALLY, and\nits docstring concedes why: \"The separator is therefore not reliable evidence of\na package boundary. What *is* reliable is Go's naming convention.\" That\ninference is Go-specific; _module_matches takes no lang and serves all 15\ncatalogues. Share of catalogue modules whose FIRST component is capitalised --\ni.e. where the discriminator carries no information at all: haskell 100%,\nswift 97%, objc 95%, elixir 52%, javascript 21%, everything else 0%.\n\nFIRST STEP, and do not skip it: decide whether the fix is (a) a capitalisation\ntest on arm 3 symmetric with arm 2, (b) a lang-aware predicate, or (c) removing\nthe orthographic inference entirely in favour of a declared type/namespace\ndistinction on the row (see the module-key axis proposal). (a) is the smallest\nand is NOT obviously right -- it would also drop the legitimate go\n`filepath` <- `path/filepath` and `grpc` <- `google.golang.org/grpc` matches\nmeasured in the same run, which are arm 3 doing its intended job. Measure the\nremoval against those before shipping: this arm carries true positives."  # db96
    fields:  # db96
      statement: "A catalogue row must not be reachable from a module hint that names something other than the row's own module or receiver type. VIOLATED: github.com/gin-gonic/gin.Context.String is reachable from the bare hint 'context', so sops's context.String(\"input-type\") -- a urfave/cli flag read on a parameter named context -- classifies as net_send."  # db96
      root_cause: "_module_matches arm 3 (dropped qualification) compares trailing components with no capitalisation test, on the stated grounds that dropped components are leading namespace. When the catalogue module's trailing component is a TYPE rather than a namespace, the arm matches any hint spelled like that type -- including a local receiver variable. INV-safig's remedy (append the type to the import path) is what put a TYPE in trailing position."  # db96
